<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vali Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1819</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vali Khan was khan of the Kazakh Middle Juz from 1781 until his death in 1819, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abylai-khan">Ablai Khan</a>. Recognised by both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, he struggled to control rival sultans, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> began to deal with other claimants, recognising a rival khan in 1816. After his death Russia did not confirm a successor and abolished the khanship under Speransky's reforms of 1822.</p></div>
